---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/respiratory-therapy/foundational/cardiopulmonary-assessment">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Respiratory Therapy. You need no prior lessons — the
    anatomy and physiology you need are built here, and every assessment skill is taught
    from its physiological reason up.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace a breath and a red blood cell through the cardiopulmonary system, naming every structure and job along the way;</li>
    <li>explain ventilation, perfusion and the V/Q match — the heart of respiratory pathophysiology — and how oxygen and CO₂ actually travel;</li>
    <li>interpret an arterial blood gas with a fixed five-step method, and recognise acute versus chronic and single versus mixed disorders;</li>
    <li>perform the four classical moves of the physical examination — inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation — and say what each finding means physiologically;</li>
    <li>read a chest X-ray systematically and know what the core labs contribute;</li>
    <li>teach the standard breathing techniques — diaphragmatic, pursed-lip, incentive spirometry — that turn assessment findings into therapy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: no single value ever diagnoses. Vitals, examination, blood gas, imaging and
    labs are one triangulated argument about what the cardiopulmonary system is doing — and
    the therapist is the person who makes the argument instead of guessing.
  </p>

  <h2>The instrument panel</h2>
  <p>
    Respiratory therapy begins with assessment: reading the cardiopulmonary system from
    vitals, blood gases, imaging and physical findings. The logic is the same as any
    instrument panel — the body's gas-exchange machinery reports its status continuously, in
    numbers and signs, and the therapist's foundational skill is reading that panel correctly
    and knowing the physiology behind every needle. A patient who is breathing 28 times a
    minute with a low blood pressure and a silent left chest is not "sick in general"; they
    are telling you something specific about their lungs and circulation, and the assessment
    toolkit is how you hear them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="triage the panel — work the silent chest" widget="branch" config={cpaSilentBranch} />
  <p>
    The blood gas is the discipline's signature test: a few numbers that describe how well
    the lungs are exchanging oxygen and clearing CO₂, and how the body's chemistry is
    coping. But the gas is only readable on top of anatomy and physiology — you must know
    what the alveolus does, what haemoglobin carries, and how the kidneys answer the lungs
    before any of the numbers mean anything. That is why the foundational unit pairs them:
    cardiopulmonary anatomy and physiology first, then the interpretation skills built over
    it, then imaging and labs as the wider evidence base.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything here also frames the profession's identity: assessment is the therapy's
    aiming system. Every later unit — oxygen devices, ventilation modes, weaning — adjusts
    therapy against this assessment, and a ventilator change without a blood-gas rationale
    is a guess. The profession's value is exactly that it doesn't guess.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="reading an arterial blood gas, systematically" widget="equationStep" config={abgLearnCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps form a decision tree, not a list to
    memorise — each step's answer decides which branch you take at the next. Step 1 sorts
    emergencies into two families; steps 2 and 3 assign each family two suspects (lungs and
    chemistry); step 4 catches the culprit; step 5 asks whether the bystander is behaving.
    Therapists drill the sequence until it runs in seconds, because in an emergency the
    classification <em>is</em> the treatment plan: an acidotic gas with a sky-high CO₂
    points at ventilation support, while the same pH with a normal CO₂ sends you hunting
    for a metabolic cause.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> assessment is reading an instrument panel, and the blood gas is
    its centrepiece. To read it you need the machinery it describes — so follow a breath.
  </p>

  <h2>Cardiopulmonary anatomy and physiology</h2>
  <p>
    Follow a breath. Air enters through conducting airways (trachea, bronchi, bronchioles)
    that warm, humidify and filter it but exchange no gas — about 150 mL of this
    <em>anatomic dead space</em> must be re-filled with every breath before fresh air
    reaches the alveoli. At the alveoli, some 300 million thin-walled sacs wrapped in
    capillaries, the membrane separating air from blood is under a micrometre thick: oxygen
    diffuses down its gradient into blood, CO₂ diffuses out, and the whole exchange
    completes in about a quarter of a second. The muscle of breathing is the diaphragm;
    contracting it drops thoracic pressure, air flows in, and relaxation plus elastic recoil
    pushes it out — which is why a patient whose lungs have lost elasticity (emphysema) must
    <em>work</em> to exhale.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="order the breath, step by step" widget="order" config={cpaBreathOrder} />
  <p>
    Two circulations matter and they answer different questions. <strong>Ventilation</strong>
    is air reaching alveoli; <strong>perfusion</strong> is blood reaching them. Their match
    — the V/Q ratio — is the heart of respiratory pathophysiology: blood flowing past
    unventilated alveoli (pneumonia, atelectasis) is a <em>shunt</em>, oxygen-resistant; air
    reaching unperfused alveoli (pulmonary embolism) is dead-space ventilation. The body's
    elegant partial fix is hypoxic vasoconstriction, steering blood away from poorly
    ventilated regions.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file the V/Q failures" widget="sort-buckets" config={cpaVQSort} />
  <p>
    Transport completes the physiology. Oxygen travels mostly bound to haemoglobin — each
    gram carries about 1.34 mL of O₂, so arterial blood holds near 20 mL per 100 mL, versus
    under 1 mL dissolved — and the binding is drawn as the S-shaped oxyhaemoglobin
    dissociation curve: a flat plateau (saturation barely falls until PaO₂ drops below about
    60 mmHg) protecting loading, and a steep descent in tissue unloading O₂ where it is
    needed. Acid, heat and CO₂ all shift the curve right (easier unloading), which links the
    two gases' transport. CO₂ travels mostly as bicarbonate: it enters red cells, carbonic
    anhydrase converts it to carbonic acid, which dissociates — and this chemistry is why
    CO₂ is both the metabolic currency and the acid–base lever the blood gas reads.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="place where the plateau breaks" widget="place" config={cpaCurvePlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> breathing hard fixes oxygen problems. It seems natural —
  more air in, more oxygen in. But ventilation moves CO₂ fast, while oxygen uptake is
  perfusion-limited and haemoglobin-limited: a patient hyperventilating on 100 mL of blood
  bypassing their alveoli (a shunt) will not raise their PaO₂ much at all. That asymmetry
  is also why the ABG can separately implicate the lungs (CO₂, ventilation) and the
  metabolism (HCO₃⁻) — a diagnostic elegance the therapist works with every shift.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — the hyperventilation trap" widget="judge" config={cpaHardJudge} />

  <h2>Blood gas interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    The arterial blood gas reports four principal values — pH (normal 7.35–7.45), PaCO₂
    (35–45 mmHg), HCO₃⁻ (22–26 mEq/L) and PaO₂ — and the interpretation method is the fixed
    five-step habit from the widget above, drilled until automatic. First, pH names the
    crisis (acidaemia or alkalaemia). Second, PaCO₂ is the respiratory term: above 45 means
    underventilation holding an acid load; below 35 means the lungs are blowing off acid.
    Third, HCO₃⁻ is the metabolic term: below 22 is metabolic acid, above 26 a metabolic
    alkalosis. Fourth, match the offending value's direction to the pH — the matching
    abnormality is the <em>primary</em> disorder, the other term is <em>compensating</em>.
    Fifth, check whether the compensation is the right size; if not, a second disorder is
    hiding.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the normal ranges" widget="cloze" config={cpaRangesCloze} />
  <p>
    Work one. pH 7.34, PaCO₂ 65 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 35 mEq/L: pH is low (acidaemia), CO₂ is high in
    the same direction, so the primary disorder is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong> —
    underventilation (think COPD exacerbation, opiate sedation, neuromuscular weakness). But
    the bicarbonate is also high, and the kidneys only raise HCO₃⁻ meaningfully over days:
    this is <em>chronic</em> respiratory acidosis with renal compensation, the signature of
    a long-standing CO₂ retainer, and it changes therapy — this patient's drive to breathe
    may partly depend on their hypoxia, so correcting their oxygen too enthusiastically is
    dangerous. Now try pH 7.22, PaCO₂ 60, HCO₃⁻ 24: same high CO₂, but a normal bicarbonate
    — an <em>acute</em> respiratory acidosis, hours old, and an emergency that needs
    ventilation now rather than adaptation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="blood gas values → acid–base classification" widget="sliderLab" config={abgCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the sliders through the four classic pictures —
    respiratory acidosis (high CO₂ dragging a low pH), respiratory alkalosis (low CO₂
    lifting a high pH), metabolic acidosis (low bicarbonate dragging the pH down), metabolic
    alkalosis (high bicarbonate lifting it) — and watch the classification logic snap into
    place every time: the abnormality pointing in the pH's direction names the primary
    disorder. Then push the compensating value beyond its expected range and notice the
    "expected compensation" readout object: that objection is how mixed disorders are
    found.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> compensation "fixes" the disorder. It is a reasonable
  reading of the word — but compensating systems push pH back toward normal without ever
  fully normalising it, and each system has limits and timescales: lungs compensate
  metabolic disorders within minutes, kidneys compensate respiratory disorders over days.
  A fully normal pH with wild gas values is the signature of a mixed disorder, not
  health.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the machinery is mapped and the gas is read. The rest of the
    panel — hands, ears, images and labs — exists to confirm, refute and localise what the
    gas suggests.
  </p>

  <h2>The physical examination: four moves, many answers</h2>
  <p>
    Before any number, the therapist assesses by hand and ear, and the four classical moves
    each have a physiological reason. <strong>Inspection</strong>: breathing rate, depth and
    pattern (a patient using neck muscles and flaring nostrils is in respiratory distress);
    chest shape (a barrel chest suggests hyperinflation); cyanosis and clubbing of the
    fingers (chronic hypoxaemia's signatures). <strong>Palpation</strong>: chest expansion
    compared side to side (asymmetry points at collapse, effusion or pneumonia on the quiet
    side), tactile fremitus (vibration of spoken words felt through the chest — increased
    over consolidated lung, decreased over fluid). <strong>Percussion</strong>: tapping the
    chest and listening to the ring — hollow over normal air-filled lung, dull over
    consolidation or fluid, drumlike (tympanic) over a pneumothorax. <strong>Auscultation</strong>:
    breath sounds through the stethoscope — vesicular (normal), bronchial (harsh, over
    consolidation), absent (over fluid or air), and the added sounds: crackles (fluid in
    airways — oedema, pneumonia, fibrosis), wheezes (narrowed airways — asthma, COPD), and
    the pleural friction rub (inflamed pleura grating). The examination narrows the
    hypothesis before the imaging and the gas confirm it: dull percussion plus bronchial
    breath sounds plus crackles is pneumonia's signature long before the chest film.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the four moves audited" widget="spot-error" config={cpaExamSpot} />
  <p>
    Because the unit is <em>cardio</em>pulmonary, the examination extends to the circulation
    that feeds the lungs. Listening over the heart, the two normal sounds are S1 ("lub") and
    S2 ("dub") — the closing of the valves that open and close with each beat, marking the
    start and end of systole; a counted apical pulse should sit between 60 and 100 beats per
    minute and regular in a resting adult. Perfusion has its own bedside signs: skin that is
    warm and dry versus cool and clammy, capillary refill (press a fingernail; colour should
    return within about two seconds), and the strength of peripheral pulses. Neck veins
    risen above their usual fill (jugular venous distension) point at right-heart strain or
    fluid overload. A therapist who reads these signs alongside breath sounds is assessing
    one connected system, not two.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> cyanosis is an early, reliable alarm. It appears only
  once roughly 5 g per decilitre of haemoglobin is desaturated — a late sign, invisible in
  the anaemic patient (too little haemoglobin to colour the skin) and misleading in the
  polycythaemic one (blue-looking despite adequate oxygen). A blue tinge confirms trouble;
  the oximeter and the blood gas are what find it early.</p>

  <h2>Chest imaging and labs</h2>
  <p>
    The chest X-ray is the workhorse image, read systematically: airway, bones, cardiac
    silhouette, diaphragms, lung fields, and everything else (tubes, lines, devices). Its
    physiological logic is density — air is black, tissue is grey, metal is white — so
    <em>infiltrates</em> (pneumonia, oedema, blood) whiten lung fields; a <em>pneumothorax</em>
    shows as a black, vessel-free zone with a pleural edge; hyperinflated, flattened
    diaphragms betray obstructive disease; an enlarged heart silhouette starts the cardiac
    conversation. CT adds cross-sectional detail — pulmonary emboli (CT pulmonary
    angiography), interstitial disease patterns, tumour staging — and ultrasound is
    increasingly bedside-first for pleural effusion and pneumothorax.
  </p>
  <p>
    Labs complete the picture and cross-check the gas. Haemoglobin determines oxygen
    content, so anaemia can leave a patient breathless with normal saturations;
    electrolytes travel with acid–base disorders (chloride gaps, potassium shifts); BNP
    points at cardiac causes of breathlessness; troponin flags myocardial injury; D-dimer
    helps exclude embolism in low-risk patients; sputum cultures and inflammatory markers
    (WBC, CRP, procalcitonin) shape infection stories. And the assessment habit that ties
    it all together repeats: no single value diagnoses — vitals, examination, gas, imaging
    and labs are one triangulated argument about what the cardiopulmonary system is doing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each lab with what it flags" widget="match" config={cpaLabsMatch} />

  <h2>From assessment to therapy: teaching better breathing</h2>
  <p>
    Assessment earns its keep the moment it changes the patient, and the most common first
    prescriptions are breathing techniques the therapist teaches directly.
    <strong>Diaphragmatic breathing</strong> helps the tachypnoeic patient — fast, shallow,
    chest-driven breathing, whether from illness or anxiety: one hand on the chest, one on
    the abdomen, attention redirected to breathing from the diaphragm, and both rate and
    anxiety fall as the chemistry of hyperventilation settles.
    <strong>Pursed-lip breathing</strong> is the COPD classic: inhale through the nose,
    exhale slowly through puckered lips. Prolonging the expiration creates a small
    positive pressure in the airways that props them open, so more stale air leaves,
    air trapping falls, and gas exchange improves — many patients discover it
    instinctively, and teaching it makes it reliable. <strong>Incentive spirometry</strong>
    is the post-surgical workhorse: sitting upright, the patient takes a slow, deep breath
    through a device that rises with volume, holds it a few seconds, and repeats roughly
    ten times an hour while awake — deliberate deep inflation to reopen the collapsed
    alveoli (atelectasis) that surgery and pain-relieved shallow breathing encourage.
    Plain <strong>coughing and deep breathing</strong> rounds out the set. Each technique
    is simple; each exists because an assessment finding — rapid shallow pattern, air
    trapping, low volumes — named the problem it fixes.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Gas: pH 7.31, PaCO₂ 28 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 14 mEq/L. Which value matches the pH's direction, and what is the primary disorder?</li>
    <li>Why does a patient with emphysema have to work to breathe <em>out</em> while a healthy person does not?</li>
    <li>Percussion over the lower left chest is dull and breath sounds there are absent. What is occupying the space where air should be?</li>
    <li>A post-operative patient keeps taking shallow breaths because deep breaths hurt. What technique and mechanism prevent the collapsed alveoli that follow?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) HCO₃⁻ is low (under 22) in the same direction as the low
    pH, while the CO₂ is low in the opposite direction — so this is <em>metabolic
    acidosis</em> with respiratory compensation: the lungs are blowing off CO₂ to push the
    pH back up. Check the compensation's size; if it is more or less than expected, a second
    disorder hides. (2) Healthy exhalation is passive — elastic recoil pushes air out. Lost
    elasticity means the patient must actively squeeze air out, and narrowed airways trap
    more of it: the pursed-lip technique props those airways open to let more air escape.
    (3) Fluid or solid tissue — dullness means density where air should be, and absent
    sounds mean no ventilation reaching it; effusion, consolidation or collapse are the
    candidates the film will sort out. (4) Incentive spirometry — slow, deep, sustained
    inflation reopening alveoli before atelectasis establishes itself; deep breathing and
    coughing every hour serve the same goal without the device.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Assessment is the aiming system of therapy: no single value diagnoses, so vitals, examination, gas, imaging and labs are triangulated into one argument.</li>
    <li>Anatomy runs the panel: 150 mL of dead space, a quarter-second of alveolar exchange, and a diaphragm whose work depends on elastic recoil.</li>
    <li>Ventilation–perfusion matching is the heart of pathophysiology: shunt is oxygen-resistant, dead space wastes ventilation, and the gas exposes which side failed.</li>
    <li>Oxygen rides haemoglobin on an S-shaped curve; CO₂ rides bicarbonate — which makes CO₂ the acid–base lever.</li>
    <li>The ABG method is five fixed steps: pH names the crisis, CO₂ and HCO₃⁻ name the suspects, direction-match names the primary disorder, and unexpected compensation reveals a mixed state.</li>
    <li>The four exam moves — inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation — each have a physical reason, and perfusion signs (S1/S2, capillary refill, jugular veins) complete the cardiopulmonary picture.</li>
    <li>Diaphragmatic breathing, pursed-lip breathing and incentive spirometry turn assessment findings into first-line therapy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Everything later in the programme adjusts against this lesson. Oxygen devices are chosen
    by the gas and the diagnosis; ventilation modes by the acid–base state and the work of
    breathing; weaning trials by the spontaneous parameters the assessments track. The next
    lessons build those therapeutic skills on this foundation — and when a decision feels
    uncertain, the habit this page installs is always the same: go back to the panel, and
    measure again.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The alveolar gas equation quantifies the gas exam's hardest question</strong> —
    is the hypoxaemia from hypoventilation alone or from lung pathology? Alveolar PO₂
    (PAO₂) is computed from inspired oxygen and CO₂: on room air, PAO₂ ≈ 150 − (PaCO₂ /
    0.8) mmHg. The <strong>A–a gradient</strong> — the gap between that calculated alveolar
    value and the measured arterial PaO₂ — is small when the only problem is
    underventilation (pure hypoventilation keeps the gradient normal) and large when V/Q
    mismatch, shunt or diffusion impairment is present. That single subtraction often
    decides between "the patient just stopped breathing adequately" and "the lungs
    themselves are failing".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Non-invasive monitoring extends the gas.</strong> Pulse oximetry reads
    saturation from light absorption but has sharp edges: it says nothing about CO₂ or pH,
    reads falsely high in carbon monoxide poisoning, and loses accuracy with poor perfusion
    and dark skin pigmentation. Capnography — measuring exhaled CO₂ — has become the
    ventilator's vital sign: end-tidal CO₂ tracks alveolar ventilation beat by breath, and
    its sudden collapse is the classic signal of embolism or circuit disaster. Arterial
    sampling remains the ground truth; the modern skill is knowing which instrument answers
    which question, and when the cheap monitor is lying.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mixed acid–base states are where mastery shows.</strong> A septic patient can be
    simultaneously lactic (metabolic acidosis) and hyperventilating (respiratory alkalosis
    from both compensation and sepsis-driven drive); a COPD patient on diuretics can carry
    chronic respiratory acidosis <em>plus</em> metabolic alkalosis. Winter's formulas and
    the anion gap (normal 8–12 mEq/L) put numbers on what "expected compensation" means,
    and the therapist who runs those checks finds the second disorder that a glance at pH
    would miss.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Bedside function tests complete the assessment set.</strong> Pulse oximetry and
    the gas describe a moment; functional tests describe capacity. The classic is the
    six-minute walk: how far a patient can ambulate in six minutes at their own pace, with
    saturations and dyspnoea scored throughout — a cheap, standardised probe of exercise
    tolerance that predicts outcomes in COPD and pulmonary hypertension. Respiratory muscle
    strength (maximal inspiratory pressure) answers the neuromuscular question — can the
    diaphragm still generate adequate force? — which matters for weaning decisions. And
    cough assessment (peak cough flow) answers a question the gas never will: can the
    patient clear their own secretions, the determinant of airway safety in neuromuscular
    disease? Each test is simple; each converts a subjective complaint ("I get breathless")
    into a tracked number — and tracking numbers against interventions is the discipline's
    whole method, from first assessment to discharge.
  </p>

